 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Within the celestial, light-filled setting of St. Cyprian’s, Clarence Gate—a Victorian church next to the southern end of Regent’s Park—Bora Aksu brought a dose of bucolic fantasy to the Friday morning of London fashion week. Wafting down the aisle first came a series of pastoral brides in puff sleeves, lace-trimmed ruffles, and broderie anglaise, before a handful of more subversive elements were slowly worked in: trench coats and tailoring, then bib harnesses, apron ties, and cinched cummerbund-style sashes. Finally, a trio of looks in a deep oxblood for a touch of the Gothic, with exposed corsetry and yarn creeping across the body like tree roots, a gentle soundtrack of ’70s British psychedelic folk playing over the speakers.</p><p>It was a reminder that, for all of Aksu’s frilly, floaty femininity, there’s often a darker, more masculine edge lurking underneath. As is custom for the designer, that reason lay, in part, in a historical figure he was inspired by—in this case, the 17th-century portraitist Mary Beale, widely recognized as Britain’s first professional female painter. (It turns out those rich crimsons at the end were a shade lifted directly from her paintings.) While women were barred from formal academies and artist guilds at the time, Beale established a thriving practice painting some of the most notable figures of her day, and was her family’s primary breadwinner. “She was really a big believer in equality between men and women, and I think she showed it in her life and work,” Aksu said—hence those more masculine elements in the collection, which took inspiration from 17th-century men’s dress.</p><p>Equally compelling was the craft on display, most notably in the form of a dazzlingly intricate lace technique. Back in the spring, Aksu received an invitation from the mayor of the eastern Turkish city of Gaziantep to visit, and he discovered a historic lace technique that has been used there for over two centuries, produced solely by local women. “They basically have this silk cloth, and they pull the threads of each line to create patterns—it takes three months, six months, depending on the pattern,” Aksu explained. Typically used for bedspreads, tablecloths, and napkins, it’s the first time the textile has been used in a fashion context—and serves as a testament to both Aksu’s endless curiosity and his ability, in his warm weather collections, to create volume and theatricality without it feeling too heavy.</p></div>",
